{"title":"Involution","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>An involution is a function whose inverse is itself. That is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/6/ae61a2bd134ee630f7a24b3f3bdc44c7eeb20acb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(f(x))=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" />.\nFrom the perspective of set theory and functions, if a relation is a function and is symmetric, then it is an involution.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Involution&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>The function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/a/47a8eb9ad3294fd27ff7bc07c4859808eddecfb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y(x)=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> has the inverse <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/2/37285fe68dbc47b0b801d3081d33c6dbde47a3cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(y)=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />, which is the same function, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/7/4874544e2638f3712dbb70e5ccf37558a5fc0acb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> is an involution.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Logical_NOT&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Logical NOT (page does not exist)\">logical NOT</a> is an involution because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/0/500b1134b7defe3d95b4e83869fbd68a787c473c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\neg { \\neg p} \\equiv p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"66\" height=\"12\" />.</li>\n<li>The additive negation is an involution because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82b25eaf90554647ce268f45f8c0d0d94a0d752d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$--x=x$\" width=\"81\" height=\"8\" />.</li>\n<li>The identity function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/6/996dddce1123ccd3e2c28fcf9320bf91ce6a294c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> is an involution because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/3/ab39ff76526ca89868920a52e2df5a0bc8874907.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_x:X \\rightarrow X = \\{(x,x) | x \\in X\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"234\" height=\"20\" /> therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/0/8e0fd52c01b5806ad77d9adb42e77098478792a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall (x,x) \\in I_x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/7/427c0ee5f10895f708b681a16c4f5799b31f41a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) =  x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/7/577d36f19d8078a378cba936d43eb40151b1e13d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(f(x)) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />. Hence, it is an involution.</li>\n<li>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multiplicative_inverse\" title=\"Multiplicative inverse\">multiplicative inverse</a> is an involution because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/6/0668bc04516bf5b9c0113a5b8206ef64b491556b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x}}=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"49\" height=\"43\" />. In fact, for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/e/55e8ebdaaa5570450093c96689324caff81e5a78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\neq 0, f(x)=\\frac{n}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"125\" height=\"33\" /> is an involution.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Properties\">Properties</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Involution&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Properties\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/c/a7cdaff0308c53856fa6ae3b2bcf292804b81919.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:X \\rightarrow X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> is an involution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/7/4b7822aebffad0fc070fac8929eb45da399e369f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\iff$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"37\" height=\"10\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/d/a7d51a2b461978655b9f999b9d2c944adff26dfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall x \\in X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/4/2d40561797595400ae0720f3f4b74eda42126859.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x) = y \\land f(y) = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" />. This induces that both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/d/92d038a95751a2d4e924b13a6d754084b97e6c37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(y,x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> are in f. By the definition of the inverse of a function, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/2/58295798b1109befe02964573da4409d2656d3ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ (y,x) | (x,y) \\in f \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" /> is the inverse of the function f. Therefore, the function f must contain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/1/74153d34ed1e2096b5c034cb85eff5a695b887c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f^{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" />. From this, it is obtained that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/6/3265e79efd21e672f9d1ef0e4c91596487022560.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f^{-1} \\subseteq f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"20\" />. Simmilalry, we can show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37eb66c63ce6eb578b126fe0e0678a08b3e70264.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f \\subseteq f^{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"20\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/d/ffdbfb4314293e11da82c7ca7464dc02873517cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f = f^{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>Rewriting the first line we have: function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/c/a7cdaff0308c53856fa6ae3b2bcf292804b81919.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:X \\rightarrow X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> is an involution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/7/4b7822aebffad0fc070fac8929eb45da399e369f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\iff$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"37\" height=\"10\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/d/ffdbfb4314293e11da82c7ca7464dc02873517cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f = f^{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<ul><li>A function is an involution <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Iff\" title=\"Iff\">iff</a> it is symmetric about the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/7/4874544e2638f3712dbb70e5ccf37558a5fc0acb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> in the coordinate plane.</li>\n<li>All involutions are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bijection\" title=\"Bijection\">bijections</a>.</li>\n<li>If a function is an involution in fraction form, then it implies a circular inversion with center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a point on the number line not determined/undefined at some value of the domain of such function. This is evident in <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/1997_AIME_Problems/Problem_12?srsltid=AfmBOorv-1A5nPS7MchAfLrLUCmYugA0cfPBx2sWfiCig3tc6Dfg8f_B\">1997 AIME Q12</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Involution&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018044627\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.018 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 133/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 271/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5593/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.347      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.347      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7217:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018044627 and revision id 268451. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}